++# User-Facing Voice
 +
-+# Product Ontology
++Canonical rule for how the system addresses the user — in any checkpoint,
++summary, status update, close report, or ad-hoc conversation. It standardizes
++the register; it does not standardize the content.
 +
-+The agreed map of what a product is made of in Garura: the kinds of things, what each holds, how they connect, and what must always be true. A person and an agent reading it use the same words for the same things. It defines thirteen kinds in two layers, with the Plan as the thread between them: the **product layer** — Business Intent, Source, Domain, Capability, Feature, Slice and ICE; the **Plan**; and the **work layer** — Epic, Story, Spike, Chore and Bug. It grows one kind at a time, through the `edit-ontology` play, and the product owner agrees each change.
++This rule is guidance, not compiled scaffolding. It is loaded on every
++session in this repo via project `CLAUDE.md` Section 5 — that is the active
++enforcement surface. (Earlier the rule was also pasted into every compiled
++play with lint anchors and a `/create-play` compiler gate; that paste-block
++mechanism was reverted under #386 — see History.)
 +
-+How it flows: a person gives an intent → it becomes a **Business Intent** here and an issue labelled `product` in the tracker → the Business Intent links to its **Capabilities** and holds no **ICE**; each Capability has **Features**, and each Capability and each Feature has exactly one ICE, its own node → a **Slice** groups Features from any Capabilities, in order, into one vertical a person can test end to end; `/shape-slice` writes the link "slice serves business intent", a thin ICE for each part the slice needs that has no ICE, and the slice's hidden file, with one entry for each Feature → the plan play makes a **Plan** right after `/shape-slice`: the Plan is given the Business Intent, holds every Slice that serves it, and creates one **Epic** for each, with its Stories, and writes nothing in the evals folder → the Epic holds one **Story** for each Feature of the Slice, and keeps the map from each Feature to its Story; each Story delivers its Feature and writes its scope of work from every line of its Feature's ICE and its Capability's ICE except the "Fails if" lines → a **Spike**, **Chore** or **Bug** is a child of one Story → as work finishes, the Business Intent is updated with what was done.
++## Scope
 +
-+The links, in five lines:
++This rule covers **every reply the system makes to the user** — checkpoint
++prompts, status updates, summaries, close reports, and ad-hoc conversation
++between plays. It does **not** cover artifact templates the play instructs
++its sub-agents to produce internally (e.g. a planning `spec.md` template, a
++brief produced by `assess-pr`, or similar). Those templates are downstream
++machine-language artifacts; extending the voice rule to them was explored
++under #386 and dropped.
 +
-+1. A Plan is made by the plan play right after `/shape-slice`. It is given one Business Intent; it holds every Slice that serves that intent; it creates one Epic for each Slice, with its Stories (three actions, in this order). It writes nothing in the evals folder.
-+2. An Epic holds Stories; each Story sits inside exactly one Epic.
-+3. A Story delivers one Feature, and with it that Feature's part of its Capability.
-+4. A Spike, Chore or Bug is a child of one Story. It reaches the Feature, the Epic and the Business Intent through that Story.
-+5. Domains and Capabilities are in the product model only; no work item links to them. A Story reaches its Capability through its Feature.
++## The rule
 +
-+In the tracker, an Epic is an issue of type Epic and a Story an issue of type Story, each with no label. The label `product` is on a Business Intent only, and the label `work-item` on a Spike, a Chore or a Bug only. A Feature and a Slice are not issue types: in the tracker, a Story represents a Feature and an Epic represents a Slice. Domain, Capability, Source and ICE are in the model only; a Plan is a file. Work is the name of the layer, not a kind. The `*-change` plays act on work items only.
++When addressing the user, lead in the language the user operates in:
++**product, feature, capability, technology, outcome.** File paths, component
++names, class names, function names, and line numbers belong in an appendix or
++a machine-readable artifact — never in the lead of a human-facing message.
++
++Why: users working at L3 do not read individual files. They think and speak
++in products being built, features being delivered, technologies being used.
++When a reply opens with file paths or component tables, the user is lost —
++they have to translate the system's machine-language into the
++product-language they were already operating in. Translation is the system's
++job, not the user's.
++
++## Signals you've drifted
++
++- The opening sentence of a reply names a file path, a class, a function, or
++  a line number.
++- The user has to read a table of files to learn what the work is *about*.
++- A status update enumerates components touched before stating the outcome.
++- A checkpoint asks the user to approve "changes to X.yaml" instead of "the
++  decision that Y".
++
++When any of these signals appear in a draft reply, rewrite before showing the
++user.
++
++## What stays in machine-language
++
++Downstream agents (code-builder, test-engineer, judge, others) still need
++file-level detail to do their work. So a reply may carry two registers:
++
++- **User-facing lead** — product, feature, capability, technology, outcome.
++  This is what the user reads.
++- **Machine-facing appendix** — file paths, component names, line numbers.
++  This is what downstream agents (or the user, when they want detail)
++  consume.
++
++Both can live in the same reply. The order matters: user-facing lead first,
++machine-facing appendix second. A reply with only an appendix (no lead) is
++non-compliant; a reply with only a lead (no appendix) loses traceability
++when traceability matters. Two registers, in that order.
++
++## History
++
++- **#385** shipped the rule as a paste-the-block mechanism: a
++  `## User-Facing Voice` section between `## Role` and the next section in
++  every compiled play, HTML-comment lint anchors enforced by
++  `core/tools/lint-components/lib/rules/structural.js` (rule IDs
++  `structural/missing-user-facing-voice` and
++  `structural/user-facing-voice-order`), plus `/create-play` gate
++  `G13 — User-Facing Voice`. Scope was orchestrator-to-user surfaces only.
++- **#386** was the open follow-on to extend the same paste-the-block
++  mechanism to artifact templates inside each play. The user dropped the
++  experiment — the block-paste wasn't materially changing how the system
++  talks to the user.
++- **Revert (#386, commits `70fa56a` + `a82aabd`).** Stripped the block from
++  all 26 compiled plays, removed gate G13 and the required-section row from
++  `/create-play`, removed the lint anchor checks from `structural.js`.
++- The rule file itself was kept. Project `CLAUDE.md` Section 5 inlines the
++  short version and points here for the long form — that became the active
++  enforcement surface in place of the play-block mechanism.
 +
-+**Nothing is blocked** (the product owner, 2026-10-21). A plan can be built and worked on before the product model is complete; the model grows as plans are built, and `/discover-domains` and `/discover-capabilities` fill the gaps later.
