+++++# Rules
++ ++
++-++A plan's file is `<plans-dir>/<plan-id>.yaml` (ontology v5, Plan): its ID is its business
++-++intent's issue number, and its `tree` lists every capability and functionality under it,
++-++each with its `issue`. This script finds the plan whose tree holds the issue (or whose ID is
++-++the issue) and names the branches:
+++++Canonical rules for every recipe-producing domain. Each file holds the single source of truth for "how must artifacts of this type look" or "what operational rules apply to this surface". Skills reference these files instead of inlining constraints in their own prose.
++ ++
++-++  plan branch   plan/<plan-id>/base
++-++  issue branch  plan/<plan-id>/<issue>-<slug>      (an issue in a plan)
++-++                feature/<issue>-<slug>             (an issue in no plan — a one-off fix)
+++++Agents and skills query this category when they need to know: **"What rules must this artifact or surface follow?"**
++ ++
++-++    python3 resolve_plan.py --plans-dir .garura/project/plans/ --issue N --slug <slug> \
++-++        [--out <path.json>]
+++++## Contents
++ ++
++-++Prints { issue, plan_id, plan_branch, issue_branch }; plan_id and plan_branch are null for
++-++an issue in no plan. Exit 0 found or not found, 2 when a plan file cannot be read, or when
++-++the issue sits in more than one plan. Read only: no git, no network, no LLM.
++-++"""
++-++import argparse
++-++import glob
++-++import json
++-++import os
++-++import sys
+++++### Naming
++ ++
++-++import yaml
+++++| Path | Description | Consumers |
+++++|------|-------------|-----------|
+++++| `naming.md` | How plays, loops, skills and agents are named — verb first, one meaning per verb, no tool built-ins, loops `complete-<phase>` (ADR 046) | every builder of a harness part, `create-play`, `create-loop` |
+++++| `naming.yaml` | The machine half: the verb lists, industry terms, reserved built-in commands | `core/tools/lint-components` |
+++++| `rename-map.yaml` | Every old name and its new name, from adopting the standard. A name mapped to `null` was removed with no successor (ADR 059): the old-name check still reports it, and the install has nothing to lay for it | the old-name check, `install-garura` |
++ ++
+++++### Knowledge rules
++ ++
++-++def issues_in(nodes):
++-++    for node in nodes or []:
++-++        if isinstance(node, dict):
++-++            if node.get("issue") is not None:
++-++                yield str(node["issue"])
++-++            yield from issues_in(node.get("children"))
+++++| Path | Description | Consumers |
+++++|------|-------------|-----------|
+++++| `resolution.md` | R1-R4 LTM resolution protocol — LTM hierarchy enforcement, authority semantics (LOCKED / DRAFT), resolution trace schema, context isolation exemptions; the decision trace every agent keeps (ADR 035 §6, ADR 037) | `tech-designer`, `repo-orchestrator`, every agent that reads LTM |
++ ++
+++++### Eval rules
++ ++
++-++def resolve(plans_dir, issue, slug):
++-++    issue = str(issue)
++-++    found = []
++-++    for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(plans_dir, "*.yaml"))):
++-++        if os.path.basename(path) == "index.yaml":
++-++            continue
++-++        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
++-++            plan = yaml.safe_load(fh) or {}
++-++        if not isinstance(plan, dict):
++-++            raise ValueError(f"{path} is not a mapping")
++-++        pid = str(plan.get("id", os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(path))[0]))
++-++        if issue == pid or issue in set(issues_in(plan.get("tree"))):
++-++            found.append(pid)
++-++    if len(found) > 1:
++-++        raise ValueError(f"issue {issue} sits in more than one plan: {', '.join(found)}")
++-++    pid = found[0] if found else None
++-++    if pid:
++-++        return {"issue": issue, "plan_id": pid, "plan_branch": f"plan/{pid}/base",
++-++                "issue_branch": f"plan/{pid}/{issue}-{slug}"}
++-++    return {"issue": issue, "plan_id": None, "plan_branch": None,
++-++            "issue_branch": f"feature/{issue}-{slug}"}
+++++| Path | Description | Consumers |
+++++|------|-------------|-----------|
+++++| `eval-format.md` | The rules of a slice's eval (ADR 057): its Gherkin story files and slice file, the tags and the Lines covered list, the fixed yardstick (a later run only adds; a moved story line is named for a person, never rewritten), and what an eval never holds | `/shape-eval`, `author-slice-eval`, the eval runner (later) |
