+++# ADR — Decision Record Template
+ +
+-+Single source of truth for **who** records run evidence, **when**, and **how it
+-+is switched on or off**. This is the D1 auto-build rule (#434): every play
+-+`/create-play` compiles emits evidence the same way, so the rule lives here
+-+once instead of being re-decided per play.
+++> The one template every decision record is written from (#632). A decision record IS an ADR,
+++> at any level (`schemas/product-os/decision.yaml`). It has **two shapes over the same core
+++> fields**:
+++>
+++> - **Markdown** — Garura's own ADRs, `docs/adr/NNN-<slug>.md`, read by people.
+++> - **YAML** — product decisions, a `decision.yaml` record in the product model, written by the
+++>   plays that make them. YAML records are machine data: a forward pointer or an acceptance
+++>   rewrites the record, and comments in it are not kept.
+++>
+++> The `/record-decision` play and the `author-decision` skill write from this template; `adr.py lint` (in the `author-decision`
+++> skill) checks a record against it. It checks the shape, never the merit — whether a decision is *right* is a
+++> person's call (`knowledge/review/adrs.md`). Built from `decision.yaml`, that review playbook,
+++> and the shape ADRs 029–031 share. ADRs written before it stay as they are until they are
+++> reviewed (#632, A4).
+ +
+-+It governs the machine-readable evidence file (the `C1` slot of the Standard
+-+Play Close). It does not change the user-facing delivery report (`C2`), which is
+-+a UX surface and is never gated — see `_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays).
+++## Core fields — both shapes
+ +
+-+## The rule
+++| Field | What it holds | Required |
+++|-------|---------------|----------|
+++| `id` | Markdown: the three-digit number (`031`). YAML: the record id | yes |
+++| `title` | The decision in one line, as a statement — not a topic | yes |
+++| `status` | `proposed`, `accepted`, `superseded` or `rejected`. Only a person moves it to `accepted` or `rejected` | yes |
+++| `date` | When it was decided (accepted), or proposed | yes |
+++| `level` | `framework`, `product`, `capability` or `feature` | yes |
+++| `node_ref` | The part of the product it affects; empty for a framework ADR | product levels |
+++| `accepted_by` | The PERSON who accepted it; empty while proposed. Markdown: the **Decided by:** line. YAML: `metadata.accepted_by` (YAML's `metadata.decided_by` keeps its original meaning: the play that recorded it) | once accepted |
+++| `decided_in` | Where it was decided — the issue, the run, the review | yes |
+++| `supersedes` | Older ADRs it replaces, in full or in part (each with what part) | when any |
+++| `amends` | Older ADRs it changes in part, and what part | when any |
+++| `superseded_by` / `amended_by` | The forward pointers — the ONLY fields ever written on an accepted ADR after it is accepted; a record is superseded in full only once | when any |
+++| `affects` | The files, rules, schemas, plays and agents the decision changes | yes |
+++| `ice_impact` | The agent intents (ICE) the decision changes, or `none` — and why | yes |
+++| `business_intent_check` | `none` — no business intent changes; `kind` — it changes the Business Intent *kind* (framework doctrine), no person's intent; `instance` — it would change a person's business intent: the person is asked, it is never applied by the ADR | yes |
+++| `related` | ADRs and issues to read with it | no |
+++| `context` | Why a decision is needed now: the situation and the forces | yes |
+++| `decision` | What is decided, in plain words, numbered when there are several parts | yes |
+++| `consequences` | What gets better and what gets harder or riskier | yes |
+++| `alternatives` | Each option not taken, and why not | yes |
+++| `for_the_accepter` | What the person who accepts it must know before deciding: each point found on the way that the person did not ask about. Each point names what it is and where, the detail of what is wrong or worth knowing, why it matters and for whom, what this decision does about it, and what the person decides | when any |
+++| `work_this_creates` | Follow-up work the decision opens | no |
+++| `references` | Sources: decisions, issues, files | no |
+++
+++**Rules**
+++
+++- An accepted ADR is never edited. A new ADR supersedes or amends it, and the older one gains
+++  only its forward pointer (`superseded_by` / `amended_by`) and, when superseded in full, the
+++  status `superseded`.
+++- Every ADR it supersedes or amends names it back through that pointer — both directions. In
+++  the YAML shape `superseded_by` holds the one full supersede; an amendment or a supersede in
+++  part points back through `amended_by`, which can hold many.
+++- Every Markdown ADR is listed in `docs/adr/_index.md`; `/record-decision` rebuilds the index after each
+++  acceptance (`adr.py index`).
+++- An ADR never changes a person's business intent. `business_intent_check: instance` stops the
+++  ADR from being applied and goes to the person (ADR 031: only a person confirms or drops a
+++  business intent).
+++- Inside a loop, an ADR is written as `proposed` and accepted at the loop's final review; run
+++  by hand, the person accepts it before it is written as `accepted`.
+++- A point for the person who accepts it is written for someone who was not in the work. It
+++  names the thing by its plain name and its place, and gives the detail itself (the lines, the
+++  names, the dates), not a summary. A point that only says "the page is out of date" or "the
+++  agent broke a rule", without which page, out of date how, which agent or which rule, is not
+++  finished.
+ +
+-+**Evidence recording is a play-only responsibility.** A play is the only
+-+component that may write a run-evidence file. Agents and skills never write
+-+evidence — not their own, not the play's.
+++## Shape 1 — Markdown (Garura's ADRs)
+ +
+-+- **Plays** record evidence, as the final step of the run, via the Standard
+-+  Play Close (`_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays)). Nothing else in the run does.
+-+- **Agents** craft context and write their working artifacts to STM (analysis,
+-+  manifests, audits). Those are inputs the play's evidence file *points at* —
+-+  they are not evidence files and are not gated by this rule.
+-+- **Skills** produce their named output artifact and return. A skill never
+-+  opens an evidence file, never stamps a run summary, never decides whether
+-+  evidence is on.
+++File: `docs/adr/NNN-<slug>.md`. Headings in this order; header lines are bold field lines.
+ +
+-+Why play-only: evidence is the record of one *run* of one *play*. Only the play
+-+knows the run boundaries (start, halt, abort), the full artifact set, the
+-+checkpoint decisions, and the eval results. An agent or skill sees one slice and
+-+would record a partial, duplicated, or conflicting account. Centralizing it in
+-+the play keeps exactly one evidence file per run, written by the one component
+-+that sees the whole run.
+++```markdown
+++# ADR NNN — <Title as a statement>
+ +
+-+## The switch — config-driven, tunable per play
+++**Status:** Proposed | Accepted | Superseded by [ADR MMM](MMM-<slug>.md) | Rejected
+++**Date:** YYYY-MM-DD
+++**Level:** framework | product | capability | feature
+++**Decided by:** <person> · **Decided in:** #<issue> (<what kind of work>)
+++**Supersedes:** ADR MMM (<in full | in part — what part>) · …            (omit when none)
+++**Amends:** ADR MMM — <what part> · …                                      (omit when none)
+++**Affects:** <files, rules, schemas, plays, agents>
+++**ICE impact:** <the agent intents it changes, or "none — <why>">
+++**Business intent check:** none | kind — <which kind> | instance — <flagged to the person>
+++**Related:** <ADRs and issues>                                              (optional)
+ +
+-+Evidence recording fires on a config setting, with a global default and an
+-+optional per-play override.
+++## Context
+ +
+-+**Global default.** `.garura/core/config.yaml`:
+++<Why a decision is needed now.>
+ +
+-+```yaml
+-+evidence:
+-+  record: false        # global default for the C1 evidence-file write
+-+```
+++## For the Person Who Accepts This                                         (when any)
+ +
+-+When `record` is false, plays skip the C1 evidence-file write and record
+-+`evidence skipped (record=false)` in the delivery report's pointer line. The C2
+-+delivery report is still emitted — it is never gated.
+++### <The point, as a plain statement>
+ +
+-+**Per-play override.** A single global flag is too blunt: a pilot may want
+-+evidence off everywhere but on for the one play under test, or on everywhere but
+-+off for a noisy high-frequency play. An optional per-play map overrides the
+-+global default for that play only:
+++- **What and where:** <its plain name and its place: a file, a page, an agent, a tier>
+++- **What is wrong, or worth knowing:** <the detail itself: the lines, the names, the dates>
+++- **Why it matters:** <what goes wrong, and for whom>
+++- **What this decision does:** <what changes, or "nothing — <why>">
+++- **What you decide:** <the choice left to the person, or "nothing">
+ +
+-+```yaml
+-+evidence:
+-+  record: false        # global default
+-+  plays:
+-+    check-change: true   # this play records even though the global is off
+-+    status: false         # this play never records even if the global is on
+-+```
+++## Decision
+ +
+-+Resolution precedence the play applies at pre-flight (first match wins):
+++<What is decided. Number the parts when there are several: "### 1. …">
+ +
+-+1. `evidence.plays.<this-play-name>` if the key is present — use it.
+-+2. else `evidence.record` (the global default).
+-+3. else treat as `true` (record) — absence of config is not a reason to lose
+-+   evidence.
+++## Consequences
+ +
+-+The resolved value is what the Standard Play Close C1 gate reads. Sub-plays
+-+follow `_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays): a sub-play (carries `parent_run_id`) emits only C1 under
+-+this same resolved flag and skips C2; the parent's close absorbs the result. A play run inside a
+-+loop (it carries the loop contract's `loop_run_id`, not a `parent_run_id`) closes the same
+-+way: C1 under this flag, no C2, a compact result for the loop (`loop-contract.md`).
+++### Positive
+ +
+-+## Relationship to the Standard Play Close
+++- <…>
+ +
+-+This rule sets the *policy* (who records, and the on/off switch). `_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays)
+-+is the *mechanism* (the exact C1/C2 block a play emits). They converge:
+++### Negative / Risks
+ +
+-+- `_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays) C1 already honors `evidence.record`; this rule generalizes
+-+  that single flag into the global-plus-per-play resolution above.
+-+- The lint anchors and the always-on C2 report are unchanged — owned by
+-+  `_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays), not restated here.
+++- <…>
+ +
+-+## Enforced
+++## Alternatives Considered
+ +
+-+- `core/tools/lint-components` already asserts every compiled play carries the Standard
+-+  Play Close block (the C1/C2 anchors). That is the structural guarantee a play
+-+  *has* an evidence step.
+-+- This rule adds the policy check: a compiled play must read the resolved
+-+  evidence flag (global + per-play) at pre-flight and gate C1 on it — never
+-+  hard-code the write, never gate C2. A play that writes evidence outside the
+-+  close block, or that delegates evidence writing to an agent or skill, fails
+-+  this rule.
+-+- Agents and skills carry no evidence-file write at all; a skill or agent that
+-+  writes a run-evidence file is a violation regardless of config.
+++- **<Option>** — <why not>.
+ +
+-+## Related
+++## Work This Creates                                                         (optional)
+ +
+-+- `_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays) — the C1/C2 close block this rule switches on/off
+-+- `templates/evidence-file.md` — the C1 machine-readable shape
+-+- `templates/delivery-report.md` — the C2 user-facing shape (never gated)
+-+- `feedback_recipe_changes_via_rebake` — intent vs non-intent boundary
