+++# Work Plan Format
+ +
+-+| Phase | Verb | Example |
+-+|-------|------|---------|
+-+| Product → Intent | `discover` | `discover-domains` |
+-+| Intent → Design | `shape` | `shape-slice` |
+-+| Design → Code | `build` | `build-epic` |
+-+| Code → Validation | `validate` | `validate-epic` |
+-+| Validation → Run | `release` | `release-increment` |
+-+| Run → Product | `learn` | `learn-outcomes` |
+++Canonical format for a **work plan** — the human's interface to a business intent (ADR 030; `docs/philosophy/idsd.md`). It says **what** gets done, **in what order**, and **why that order**, in words a business reader can follow. It never says how.
+ +
+-+Change plays use `start`, `commit`, `propose`, `check`, `merge`, `fix`, `refactor`; plan plays
+-+`draft`, `show`, `update`, `list`; `record` writes a decision; harness plays `create`, `edit`,
+-+`install`, `uninstall`.
+++Not to be confused with the **build plan** that `/implement` writes (`specs/implement/plan.yaml`, via `author-build-plan`): that one breaks an epic into file-level pieces — it is design, for agents.
