+-+# Branch Naming Conventions
+++A forward pointer on an older Markdown ADR is written into its `**Status:**` line only:
+++`Accepted — amended in part by [ADR MMM](MMM-<slug>.md): <what changed>` or
+++`Superseded by [ADR MMM](MMM-<slug>.md)`.
+ +
+-+Standard patterns for branch names and their implications.
+++## Shape 2 — YAML (product decisions)
+ +
+-+## Branch Patterns
+++A `decision.yaml` record (the core fields above, added in #632 without changing the original ones):
+ +
+-+| Pattern | Purpose | Example |
+-+|---------|---------|---------|
+-+| `feature/*` | New feature | `feature/user-auth` |
+-+| `fix/*` | Bug fixes | `fix/login-redirect` |
+-+| `hotfix/*` | Urgent production fixes | `hotfix/security-patch` |
+-+| `release/*` | Release preparation | `release/v2.1.0` |
+-+| `experiment/*` | Exploration/POC | `experiment/new-cache` |
+-+| `spike/*` | Technical investigation | `spike/graphql-perf` |
+-+| `refactor/*` | Code improvements | `refactor/auth-module` |
+-+| `docs/*` | Documentation only | `docs/api-reference` |
+-+| `chore/*` | Maintenance tasks | `chore/upgrade-deps` |
+-+
+-+## Quality Gate Implications
+-+
+-+Branch patterns affect quality gate priorities:
+-+
+-+### Standard Branches (`feature/*`, `fix/*`)
+-+- All quality gates apply normally
+-+
+-+### Hotfix Branches (`hotfix/*`, `emergency/*`)
+-+- Some quality gates demoted to nice-to-have
+-+- Focus on critical checks only
+++```yaml
+++decision:
+++  id: <id>
+++  title: <the decision as a statement>
+++  status: proposed | accepted | superseded | rejected
+++  level: framework | product | capability | feature
+++  node_ref: <the node it affects — empty for framework or product level>
+++  reason: <context and decision, in plain words>          # v1 field: context + decision
+++  alternatives:
+++    - { name: <option>, why_not: <why> }
+++  supersedes: [ { id: <older id>, part: full | <what part> } ]
+++  amends: [ { id: <older id>, part: <what part> } ]
+++  superseded_by: <id or null>
+++  amended_by: [<ids>]
+++  affects: [<files, rules, schemas, nodes>]
+++  ice_impact: <the ICE it changes, or "none — why">
+++  business_intent_check: none | kind | instance
+++  consequences: { positive: [<…>], negative: [<…>] }
+++  for_the_accepter:                                        # when any
+++    - { point: <plain statement>, what_and_where: <name and place>, detail: <what is wrong>,
+++        why_it_matters: <what goes wrong>, this_decision_does: <what changes>, you_decide: <choice or "nothing"> }
+++  metadata:
+++    decided_by: <play that recorded it>                    # v1 meaning kept
+++    accepted_by: <person, once accepted>
+++    decided_in: <issue or run>
+++    date: <date>
+++    version: <n>
+++```
+ +
+-+### Release Branches (`release/*`)
+-+- Quality gates elevated to must-have
+-+- Stricter review requirements
+++## Gold example — Markdown
+ +
+-+### Experiment Branches (`experiment/*`, `spike/*`, `poc/*`)
+-+- Most quality gates are nice-to-have
+-+- Only essential checks (no conflicts, no secrets) enforced
+++ADR 031 (`docs/adr/031-business-intent-and-ice.md`) is the nearest written example; written
+++from this template its header would read:
+ +
+-+## Naming Best Practices
+++```markdown
+++# ADR 031 — Business Intent and ICE: Two Levels, Written Freely, Worked Only When Confirmed
+ +
+-+1. **Use lowercase** with hyphens: `feature/user-auth` not `Feature/UserAuth`
+-+2. **Keep it short** but descriptive: `fix/null-pointer` not `fix/fixing-the-null-pointer-exception-in-payment-service`
