+++# Work Plan Format
+ +
+-+Single source of truth for how a play declares where it sits in the delivery pipeline and
+-+what start/end machinery create-play folds into it. This is the D2 auto-build rule (#434):
+-+the composition lives here once, so every play create-play compiles wires the same
+-+start-change opening and the same commit → propose → review → merge closing — instead of
+-+each play re-deciding how a change is opened and landed.
+++Canonical format for a **work plan** — the human's interface to a business intent (ADR 030; `docs/philosophy/idsd.md`). It says **what** gets done, **in what order**, and **why that order**, in words a business reader can follow. It never says how.
+ +
+-+It governs only the **delivery pipeline** (the path that opens an issue, does the work, and
+-+lands it on main). Every play declares where it sits; many model-building plays now open and
+-+land their own change. As compiled today: `both` — /discover-domains, /discover-capabilities (#616), /shape-measure-lens,
+-+/shape-slice, /learn-outcomes, /fix-bug, /refactor-code, /discover-intent (#612), /record-decision (#632), /edit-ontology (#777 — a meta play that opens and lands its own change); `start` — /shape-architecture-lens, /shape-ux-lens, /build-epic; `end` —
+-+/shape-marketing-lens, /shape-run-lens, /release-epic; `none` — /shape-agentic-lens, /shape-quality-lens, /validate-epic, /release-increment,
+-+/list-focus-issues. (A `none` play runs on whatever branch is already started.) Inside a loop the
+-+opening and the end sequence are both skipped — the loop opens and lands the change — and a
+-+play knows it is inside a loop from the loop's contract (`standards/rules/loop-contract.md`;
+-+ADR 029, ADR 030 P4). Wired so far: /discover-intent (#612, its own contract check), and /discover-domains (#614) and
+-+/discover-capabilities (#615), both with the shared `check_loop_contract.py`.
+++Not to be confused with the **build plan** that `/implement` writes (`specs/implement/plan.yaml`, via `author-build-plan`): that one breaks an epic into file-level pieces — it is design, for agents.
+ +
+-+## The declaration
+++This file holds the shape only. When a work plan must exist, who writes and updates it, and when it is done are rules, in `standards/rules/work-plan.md`.
+ +
+-+Every play declares one frontmatter field:
+++## Where it lives
+ +
+-+```yaml
+-+position: start | end | both | none
+-+```
+++See `standards/rules/work-plan.md` → "Where it lives". (Kept in one place so the two files cannot drift.)
+ +
+-+- **none** (default) — standalone play; nothing is injected. Strategic/model-building
+-+  plays and one-off utilities use this.
+-+- **start** — the play opens a pipeline. create-play injects **start-change** as the
+-+  play's first step (resolve/create the issue, cut the branch off fresh main, optional
+-+  worktree, init STM).
+-+- **end** — the play closes a pipeline. create-play injects the **end sequence** as the
+-+  play's closing steps, in order: **commit-change → propose-change → check-change →
+-+  merge-change**, placed after the play's own work and before the Standard Play Close.
+-+- **both** — the play is self-contained: start-change is injected at the front and the end
+-+  sequence at the back, bracketing the play's own work. This is the "start, do everything,
+-+  close the loop" shape — e.g. a `fix-defect` play that opens the issue, fixes it, and
+-+  lands it in one command.
+++## Format
+ +
+-+There are only these positions. There is no "middle" — a play's own work simply sits
+-+between the injected start and end; the work is not a declared position.
+++````markdown
+++---
+++plan_for: {issue number}
+++kind: {business-intent | feature | story | bug | spike | chore | drive | play}
+++serves: "#{issue} — {the outcome this plan serves, in one line}"
+++status: {active | done | dropped}
+++updated: {YYYY-MM-DD}
+++now: {item number being worked — or - when status is done or dropped}
+++serves_plan: {issue number of the plan this one serves — omit for a business intent}
+++serves_item: {the item in that plan this work is — omit for a business intent}
+++---
+ +
+-+## The two sequences
+++# Plan — #{issue}: {short name of the outcome}
+ +
+-+| Sequence | Member plays (in order) | Injected when position is |
+-+|----------|-------------------------|---------------------------|
+-+| start | `start-change` | `start` or `both` |
+-+| end | `commit-change` → `propose-change` → `check-change` → `merge-change` | `end` or `both` |
+++This is a plan, not a design. It says what gets done, in what order, and why
+++that order. How each item is built is decided in its own issue.
+ +
+-+These five plays are the **members** — the building blocks. A member play declares the
+-+`position` of the sequence it belongs to (`start-change: start`; the four end plays: `end`)
+-+so create-play knows the sequence composition. **A member is never injected into itself**:
+-+create-play recognizes the members by name and injects no sequence into one of them.
+-+Everything else with a non-`none` position is a **consumer** that receives injection.
+-+"Member" means only these change plays: no step of a loop is a member play (ADR 059).
+++## What we are trying to reach
+ +
+-+## How create-play injects it (explicit, named)
+++{Plain words. What is true today, what should be true instead, and why it
+++matters. Name the key ideas a newcomer needs to read the items below.}
+ +
+-+Injection is explicit, not a hidden macro. The injected pieces appear as named sub-play
+-+steps in the compiled play, wired as JSON-contract handoffs over files on disk (the same
+-+discipline as any other step) and run as sub-plays — each is dispatched with
+-+`parent_run_id` so it emits only its C1 evidence and the parent's close absorbs it (per
+-+`_shared/play-close.md` (beside the plays)).
+++## When this plan is done
+ +
+-+- **start** → prepend a first step that runs `start-change`, before the play's own first
+-+  work step. The play's Task DAG gains that step at the head; everything else `blockedBy`
+-+  it.
